Article Text:
(From left) Navneet Singh, Dinesh Kumar, Putul Sonowal, Rupa Rani Tirkey, Pinki and Nayan Moni Saikia during their pre-CWG camp in Glasgow.

CWG Squad:

Men’s Singles: Putul Sonowal;

Men’s Pairs: Navneet Singh, Dinesh Kumar;

Women’s Singles: Nayan Moni Saikia;

Women’s Pairs: Rupa Rani Tirkey, Pinki.

KOLKATA: As the Commonwealth Games begin in Glasgow on Thursday, India’s lawn bowl players will once again hope to finish on the podium after their historic achievement in the previous edition, where the country won gold and silver medals in the women’s and men’s fours events respectively. For Nayanmoni Saikia, this CWG offers an opportunity to build on the success of Birmingham. The Assam athlete was part of the gold medal-winning team and believes the triumph changed the way the sport is perceived in the country.“Earlier, very few people knew about the sport, but after we won medals, lawn bowls started getting recognition and people became more aware of it,” Saikia told TOI.The Arjuna awardee will compete in the women’s singles this time, while her Birmingham teammate Rupa Rani Tirkey will partner Pinki in the women’s pairs after the fours event was omitted from the shortened programme. The duo will play their opening match against Malta’s Connie Rixon and Rebecca Rixon on the opening day of the Games.With only the singles and pairs events retained, several members of India’s medal-winning fours teams have had to adapt to new formats. This is why Tirkey believes communication and handling pressure will be crucial. “In pairs competition, trust is extremely important. Throughout the match, we have to constantly communicate, motivate each other and make decisions after every end about where to draw the bowls,” Tirkey said.The biggest difference this time is that the competition will be played indoors. To prepare for the conditions, the Indian contingent underwent a month-long training camp in Glasgow.Apart from the overseas training camp, several players also gained valuable exposure by competing in this year’s Asian Championships and the World Indoor Championships, including Putul Sonowal. The Assam player, who was not part of the Birmingham Games, heads to Glasgow in fine form after winning the men’s singles gold medal at the Asian meet.“Winning the gold medal at Asian Championships has given me a lot of confidence. Competing at the World Indoor Championships also gave me the opportunity to play against some of the best players in the world and interact with them,” said Sonowal, who will open his campaign against Canada’s Ryan Bester on Thursday.“They appreciated my game but also pointed out areas where I could improve. Since then, I have been working hard to address those shortcomings.”In the men’s pairs, meanwhile, the experienced Dinesh Kumar will join hands with the young Navneet Singh. Both were part of India’s silver medal-winning men’s fours team at the last CWG and combined to win men’s pairs gold at this year’s Asian Championships.“It would be too early to say much about our partnership just yet. We still have to prove ourselves on bigger stages, but I can certainly say that we are developing an excellent understanding,” said Kumar, who hails from Jharkhand.For Kumar, this will be his fifth Commonwealth Games, and he believes his experience of competing at the highest level will stand him in good stead.With India’s breakthrough performance in Birmingham having raised expectations, the squad will now be hoping to show that the success four years ago was no flash in the pan and the country can once again challenge the traditional powerhouses of the sport on the CWG stage.
